          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Full pipeline gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax(MHz). To meet >=1 MSPS we need Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially satisfied by any Artix-7 CORDIC datapath (Fmax is typically 100s of MHz). The binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy is dominated by n_iter and data_width. For a 14-bit output (LSB=2^-12), we need n_iter such that the CORDIC residual error plus truncation stays below 2^-10. Typically n_iter >= 12-14 with data_width >= 14 gives accuracy_bits > 10. I focus n_iter in [12,16] and data_width in [14,20] to capture the area/accuracy trade-off. angle_guard and frac_guard are swept to find the minimal guard bits that still meet the error constraint, since they directly add area. Rounding is included because it can reduce required guard bits."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, reducing FF count compared to full pipelined while still achieving 1 result/cycle throughput. Since the objective is min luts_plus_ffs and area is everything, pipelined_m with m>1 can significantly reduce FFs at the cost of slightly lower Fmax (longer combinational paths between registers). For a motor-control loop at >=1 MSPS, even a modest Fmax reduction is acceptable as long as Fmax stays well above 1 MHz. I sweep m in [2,4] to find the sweet spot where FF savings outweigh any Fmax penalty. Same n_iter/data_width/guard ranges as pipelined since accuracy requirements are identical."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts+ffs) as the primary objective. Both pipelined and pipelined_m families produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax in MHz. Since Fmax for a CORDIC on Artix-7 is typically 100-400 MHz, the throughput constraint is easily met by both families. The real trade-off is between area (LUTs+FFs) and accuracy. Accuracy depends on n_iter, data_width, angle_guard, frac_guard, and rounding. I focus n_iter in [12,16] and data_width in [14,20] because: (1) data_width=14 gives output LSB=2^-12, so with n_iter>=12 the CORDIC error should be well below 2^-10; (2) going below data_width=14 risks not meeting the error constraint; (3) going above data_width=20 adds area without meaningful accuracy gain for this spec. angle_guard and frac_guard are swept over their full ranges to find the minimal values that still satisfy the error constraint, since each extra guard bit adds area. Rounding is included as it can reduce required guard bits. pipelined_m is included because it can reduce FF count by not registering every stage, which directly helps the area objective. The budget is split 55/45 between pipelined and pipelined_m to give slightly more weight to the simpler full-pipeline design while still exploring the FF-reduction opportunity of pipelined_m."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
